---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { figure, immigration, zika, records } from "../../../data/rubio-said-vs-did.mjs";
---
<BaseLayout title={`${figure.title} | The Catch`} current="Figures">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/figures/">FIGURES</a><span>{figure.name}</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story">
      <p class="story-kicker"><a href="/figures/">Figures</a> &middot; {figure.name} &middot; public statements and Senate votes &middot; updated August 28, 2026</p>
      <h1>{figure.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek">{figure.dek}</p>

      <div class="kpi-strip">
        {figure.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">{k.value}</span><span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span></div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>In 2013 his exact words put border security before <em>permanent</em> status, not before any status. His five key votes sit exactly where his words did, which is also why critics who heard &ldquo;security first&rdquo; read the record differently.</li>
          <li>In 2016 he asked for the full $1.9 billion for Zika, said in advance he would take the $1.1 billion compromise if that was all he could get, and voted for both. The words and the votes match at every step.</li>
          <li>These are two complete episodes with every roll call listed, not a finding about motive, and not a claim that either episode proves a habit.</li>
        </ol>
      </aside>

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story">
        <p class="toc-label">In this story</p>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#immigration">Immigration, 2013</a></li>
          <li><a href="#zika">Zika funding, 2016</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-to-read">How to read this page</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <!-- ===================== IMMIGRATION ===================== -->
      <section id="immigration">
        <p class="sec-kicker">{immigration.topic}</p>
        <h2>Border security first. But first before <em>what</em>?</h2>

        <div class="pattern-box">
          <p class="p-name">Precise words, bigger impression</p>
          <p class="p-meaning">What it looks like: his exact words draw a careful line (&ldquo;before <em>permanent</em> status&rdquo;), while the plain hearing of them (&ldquo;security first&rdquo;) suggests a stricter promise. His votes match the careful line, not the plain hearing.</p>
          <p class="chip-row"><span class="chip chip-record">their words</span> <span class="chip chip-record">record</span> <span class="chip chip-computed">one episode, five key votes</span></p>
        </div>

        <div class="howitworked">
          <p class="hw-label">How the bill worked (read this first)</p>
          <p>The bill created a three-stage path, and a different security requirement guarded each stage:</p>
          <div class="stages">
            {immigration.stages.map((s, i) => (
              <>
                {i > 0 && <span class="stage-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>}
                <div class="stage"><p class="s-name">{s.name}</p><p class="s-gate" set:html={s.gate} /></div>
              </>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p class="note">Source: the bill's own trigger section, Section 3(c) of S.744 as passed by the Senate. <a class="src" href={immigration.billText} rel="noreferrer">Bill text</a> The whole 2013 fight, and this whole episode, is about which of those arrows the security requirements guard.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="lane said">
          <p class="lane-label">What he said</p>
          <p>Setting: the Senate floor, June 26, 2013, defending the immigration bill he had co-written to skeptical conservatives who wanted proof its border-security requirements were binding. <a class="src" href={immigration.record.url} title={immigration.record.title} rel="noreferrer">{immigration.record.label}</a></p>
          <blockquote>&ldquo;It requires and funds the completion of 700 miles of real border fence. It adds 20,000 new border agents&hellip; It requires E-Verify for every employer in America&hellip; These are all things that at a minimum <mark>must happen before those in the country illegally can apply for permanent status</mark>.&rdquo;</blockquote>
          <p class="note">Note the exact words: before <strong>permanent</strong> status. That is the green-card arrow in the diagram above, the third stage. His sentence does not promise security before the first stage, provisional status. On the diagram, his exact words are accurate.</p>
          <p class="note"><strong>Two different claims, kept separate.</strong> The sentence above is what he said on June 26, and it is what this episode tests. Separately, &ldquo;border security first&rdquo; was also the broader message around the bill, and whether that message was misleading was fought over <em>at the time</em>: critics said provisional legalization came before any security was finished; fact-checkers who compared his English and Spanish interviews found he generally described the bill's stages the same way in both, while emphasizing different stages to different audiences. <a class="src" href={immigration.politifact.url} rel="noreferrer">{immigration.politifact.label}</a> We only score the sentence he actually said against the votes he actually cast.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="lane did">
          <p class="lane-label">What he did</p>
          <ol class="votes">
            {immigration.keyVotes.map((v) => (
              <li class="vote">
                <span class="v-date">{v.date}</span>
                <span class={`cast ${v.cast}`}>Voted {v.cast}</span>
                <span class="v-text"><span set:html={"&hellip;" + v.text} /> <a class="src" href={v.url} rel="noreferrer">Roll call {v.rc}</a></span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="howitworked">
          <p class="hw-label">One thing the dates hide</p>
          <p>The bill <strong>changed</strong> between those votes and the speech. The No votes above came June 13 to 18, on a version with weaker security conditions. In the following week, the Corker-Hoeven deal made the green-card conditions much tougher: the 700 miles of fencing, roughly doubling the Border Patrol, E-Verify, new technology. The June 26 speech describes that strengthened version, and he voted for it the same day. So the sequence is: he voted down security-before-provisional-status amendments, then supported much stronger security-before-green-card requirements, then described the strengthened bill on the floor.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="verdict mixed">
          <p class="v-label">Where it lines up, and where it doesn't</p>
          <p>Measured against his exact words, his votes are consistent, and consistently placed: at every decision point, he voted to keep the security requirements on the green-card arrow and off the provisional-status arrow. He voted down each amendment that moved them earlier, and voted for the version that made the later requirements tougher.</p>
          <p>But if you heard &ldquo;border security first&rdquo; as &ldquo;before anyone here illegally gets any legal status,&rdquo; the record reads differently: he helped kill the amendments that would have made it work that way, including one demanding six months of border control before provisional status and one demanding part of the fencing be built first. The Thune amendment's fencing and the final bill's fencing were not the same requirement moved in time: Thune put part of it <em>before provisional status</em>; the bill he voted for put the fencing requirement <em>before green cards</em>. That placement is the entire dispute.</p>
          <p class="note">The refined version of this finding: he consistently resisted making <em>completed</em> enforcement a condition of provisional legalization, while supporting increasingly strict enforcement conditions on permanent residence. Whether that distinction matches what &ldquo;security first&rdquo; meant to any given listener is the open question.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="verdict strength">
          <p class="v-label">How strong is this, and what to watch out for</p>
          <p><strong>Strength:</strong> within this one episode, strong. The wording is his own, on the record, and the five votes above are the exact moments the Senate decided the question his sentence raises. The full 18-vote list below shows nothing was cherry-picked.</p>
          <p><strong>Watch out:</strong> one episode is not a habit. Careful wording is also what good lawyers and good legislators do; the same record is consistent with &ldquo;he chose his words honestly and precisely,&rdquo; and the dispute over his framing was argued in real time, not discovered later. Whether the precision was candor or cover is exactly the question we do not answer for you. If the same shape shows up on other topics, that would be worth knowing; so far we have checked this one.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cant">
          <p class="v-label">What we can't determine</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Why he voted this way.</strong> Conviction, coalition strategy, or both. We never infer motive; we show the words and the votes.</li>
            <li><strong>What he said about this bill in 2015 and 2016</strong>, when he publicly distanced himself from it. Those later statements are not yet in our searchable record. That is a coverage gap on our side, and this episode is incomplete until it is filled.</li>
            <li><strong>Whether his audiences understood the provisional-vs-permanent distinction.</strong> His words drew it explicitly; how it was heard is not something a record can show.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <dl class="gloss">
          {immigration.gloss.map((g) => (<div><dt>{g.term}:</dt><dd>{g.def}</dd></div>))}
        </dl>

        <details class="receipt votes-all">
          <summary>All 18 of Rubio's recorded votes in this episode (complete list, nothing omitted)</summary>
          <div class="tblwrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Date (2013)</th><th>Roll call</th><th>What was voted on</th><th>Rubio</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {immigration.allVotes.map((v) => (
                  <tr class={v.key ? "key" : undefined}>
                    <td class="num">{v.date}</td>
                    <td class="num"><a href={v.url} rel="noreferrer">{v.rc}</a></td>
                    <td>{v.what}{v.key && <Fragment> <span class="keymark">&#9670; key vote</span></Fragment>}</td>
                    <td>{v.rubio}</td>
                    <td class="num">{v.result}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <p class="tbl-note">{immigration.allVotesNote}</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== ZIKA ===================== -->
      <section id="zika">
        <p class="sec-kicker">{zika.topic}</p>
        <h2>&ldquo;Fully fund the $1.9 billion.&rdquo; And the votes to match.</h2>

        <div class="pattern-box">
          <p class="p-name">Words and votes in step</p>
          <p class="p-meaning">What it looks like: he asked publicly for the full emergency amount, co-sponsored the amendment providing it, and voted for it hours later. When it fell short, he voted for the smaller compromise rather than let funding fail, exactly as he said he would. One episode, not yet a pattern.</p>
          <p class="chip-row"><span class="chip chip-record">their words</span> <span class="chip chip-record">record</span> <span class="chip chip-ok">checks out</span> <span class="chip chip-computed">one episode, 16 votes</span></p>
        </div>

        <div class="howitworked">
          <p class="hw-label">The sequence (read this first)</p>
          <p>In February 2016 the White House asked Congress for roughly $1.9 billion in emergency Zika funding. By May 17 the Senate had three rival funding proposals teed up for votes on the same afternoon, including a full $1.9 billion amendment from Florida's two senators, Nelson and Rubio, and a smaller $1.1 billion bipartisan compromise from Senators Blunt and Murray. Advancing any of them required 60 votes (see &ldquo;cloture&rdquo; below). The $1.9 billion versions fell short; the $1.1 billion compromise advanced and passed. The fight then moved to House-Senate negotiations over the final bill.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="lane said">
          <p class="lane-label">What he said</p>
          <p>Setting: the Senate floor, May 17, 2016, hours before the votes, with the Zika virus spreading in Puerto Rico and Florida. <a class="src" href={zika.record.url} title={zika.record.title} rel="noreferrer">{zika.record.label}</a></p>
          <blockquote>&ldquo;I support <mark>fully funding the request</mark> made&hellip; it is really the scientists' request, the doctors' request&hellip; Why take the chance? Let's do it once. Let's get it right.&rdquo;</blockquote>
          <p>He also named the amendment as his own, and said in advance what he would do if it failed:</p>
          <blockquote>&ldquo;I am proud of the amendment that my colleague from Florida, Senator Nelson, and I are proposing here today. I hope that the $1.9 billion amount passes, but <mark>if we are left with a vote on the Blunt-Murray amendment, I think that is better than nothing, and I will support it</mark>.&rdquo;</blockquote>
        </div>

        <div class="lane did">
          <p class="lane-label">What he did</p>
          <ol class="votes">
            {zika.keyVotes.map((v) => (
              <li class="vote">
                <span class="v-date">{v.date}</span>
                <span class={`cast ${v.cast}`}>{v.castLabel ?? `Voted ${v.cast}`}</span>
                <span class="v-text"><span set:html={"&hellip;" + v.text} /> <a class="src" href={v.url} rel="noreferrer">Roll call {v.rc}</a>{v.extra?.map((e) => (<> <a class="src" href={e.url} rel="noreferrer">Roll call {e.rc}</a></>))}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="verdict aligned">
          <p class="v-label">Where it lines up</p>
          <p>The votes match the words at every step, including the hard one. He asked for $1.9 billion and voted for $1.9 billion on his own co-sponsored amendment the same day. When it fell 10 votes short of the 60 needed, he did what he had promised that morning: voted for the $1.1 billion compromise rather than oppose partial funding. Voting for $1.1 billion after asking for $1.9 billion is not a contradiction here; he stated the fallback position himself, in advance, on the record. And when the final package stalled over the summer, he voted to advance it all three times it came up.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="verdict strength">
          <p class="v-label">How strong is this, and what to watch out for</p>
          <p><strong>Strength:</strong> moderate. One episode, but a complete one: the stated ideal ($1.9 billion), a direct vote on that exact ideal, a stated fallback, and a vote matching the fallback. That is a harder test than a single aligned vote, and he passed each step of it.</p>
          <p><strong>Watch out:</strong> alignment on a pressing home-state issue (Zika was spreading in Florida) is still the easiest kind to find; words and politics pointed the same way. It cannot tell you what he does when they pull apart. The immigration episode above is that harder test.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cant">
          <p class="v-label">What we can't determine</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Whether the compromise votes reflect conviction or vote-counting. As always, we show words and votes, never motive.</li>
            <li>Voice votes and unanimous-consent actions leave no individual record, so any Zika action taken that way is invisible to a roll-call check.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <dl class="gloss">
          {zika.gloss.map((g) => (<div><dt>{g.term}:</dt><dd>{g.def}</dd></div>))}
        </dl>

        <details class="receipt votes-all">
          <summary>All 16 of Rubio's recorded votes on this bill in 2016 (complete list, nothing omitted)</summary>
          <div class="tblwrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Date (2016)</th><th>Roll call</th><th>What was voted on</th><th>Rubio</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {zika.allVotes.map((v) => (
                  <tr class={v.key ? "key" : undefined}>
                    <td class="num">{v.date}</td>
                    <td class="num"><a href={v.url} rel="noreferrer">{v.rc}</a></td>
                    <td>{v.what}{v.key && <Fragment> <span class="keymark">&#9670; key vote</span></Fragment>}</td>
                    <td>{v.rubio}</td>
                    <td class="num">{v.result}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <p class="tbl-note">{zika.allVotesNote}</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== METHOD ===================== -->
      <section id="how-to-read">
        <p class="sec-kicker">Method</p>
        <h2>How to read this page</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-record"><strong>Every sentence links to its source.</strong> Statements come from the Congressional Record, the official transcript of what is said on the Senate floor. Votes come from the Senate's official roll-call records. Bill mechanics come from the bill text itself. Open any source link to read the official record yourself. <span class="chip chip-record">record</span></p>
        <p class="railed rail-note"><strong>We never tell you what to conclude.</strong> &ldquo;He said X and voted Y&rdquo; is a finding. &ldquo;He's a hypocrite&rdquo; or &ldquo;he's principled&rdquo; is your call, not ours. We also never infer motive; the record shows what happened, not why.</p>
        <p class="railed rail-computed"><strong>What this page is built on, and its honest limits.</strong> The vote record here is essentially complete: every recorded Senate roll-call vote Rubio cast is in our system, checked against the Senate's own files. The statement record is not: of 18,444 collected documents of his public statements, about 5 percent are currently searchable. More findings will surface as that grows; until then, absence of a statement here means &ldquo;not yet searchable,&rdquo; never &ldquo;he never said it.&rdquo; <span class="chip chip-computed">computed</span></p>
        <p class="railed rail-note"><strong>How each episode's vote list was built.</strong> A statement search first connects a statement to the record; then we reconstruct the full legislative episode around it, listing every roll call on the relevant bill and amendments in the period and Rubio's position on each, so the evidence set is the episode, not just whatever the search happened to link. The complete lists above are the result.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
        <p class="sec-kicker">Sources</p>
        <h2>The records</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Everything this page rests on. Each link opens the official file.</p>
        <ul class="record-links">
          {records.map((r) => (
            <li><a href={r.url} rel="noreferrer"><span class="rl-title">{r.title}</span><span class="rl-meta">{r.what}</span></a></li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style is:global>
  @media (min-width: 1360px) { .sheet:has(.story) { max-width: 100%; } }
</style>

<style>
  .story { max-width: 52rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 1.5rem 1.25rem 4rem; }
  .story-kicker { font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #5b6470; margin: 1.5rem 0 0.75rem; }
  .story-kicker a { color: inherit; }
  .story h1 { font-family: "Newsreader", Georgia, serif; font-weight: 700; font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4.5vw, 2.7rem); line-height: 1.12; margin: 0 0 0.75rem; text-wrap: balance; }
  .story-dek { font-family: "Newsreader", Georgia, serif; font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.5; color: #3c434b; font-style: italic; margin: 0 0 1.75rem; }
  .story h2 { font-family: "Newsreader", Georgia, serif; font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.45rem; margin: 2.5rem 0 0.9rem; padding-bottom: 0.35rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #e3e0da; }
  .story h2::after { content: ""; display: block; width: 3.5rem; border-bottom: 2px solid #c98a1b; margin-bottom: -1px; margin-top: 0.35rem; }
  .story p, .story li, .story dd { font-family: "Newsreader", Georgia, serif; font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.62; }
  .story section { scroll-margin-top: 4.5rem; }
  .sec-kicker { font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #b9770e; margin: 2.4rem 0 -0.6rem; }
  .sec-kicker + h2 { margin-top: 0.8rem; }
  .section-lede { color: #5b6470; margin-top: -0.35rem; }
  .src { font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.03em; background: #eef2f6; color: #2b5474; padding: 0.08rem 0.4rem; border-radius: 2px; text-decoration: none; white-space: nowrap; vertical-align: 0.1em; }
  .src:hover { background: #dfe7ef; }
  mark { background: #fbeec8; color: inherit; padding: 0 0.1em; }

  .kpi-strip { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(140px, 1fr)); gap: 1px; background: #e3e0da; border: 1px solid #e3e0da; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .kpi { background: #fbfaf8; padding: 0.85rem 1rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.2rem; }
  .kpi-value { font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-weight: 500; font-size: 1.45rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .kpi-label { font-family: "Public Sans", system-ui, sans-serif; font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: #5b6470; }
  .three-things { border: 1px solid #e0d6c2; background: #fdfaf3; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 1rem; }
  .tt-label { font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #8a6412; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
  .three-things ol { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .three-things li { margin: 0.3rem 0; }
  .story-toc { border-top: 1px solid #e3e0da; border-bottom: 1px solid #e3e0da; padding: 0.7rem 0; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
  .toc-label { font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #5b6470; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; }
  .story-toc ol { margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.25rem 1.1rem; counter-reset: toc; }
  .story-toc li { counter-increment: toc; }
  .story-toc li::before { content: counter(toc); font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: 0.7rem; color: #b9770e; margin-right: 0.35rem; }
  .story-toc a { font-family: "Public Sans", system-ui, sans-serif; font-size: 0.85rem; color: #33404d; text-decoration: none; }
  .story-toc a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  @media (min-width: 1360px) {
    nav.story-toc { position: fixed; top: 13.5rem; left: max(1.25rem, calc(50vw - 26rem - 15.5rem)); width: 13rem; border: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; background: transparent; }
    .story-toc ol { flex-direction: column; gap: 0; border-left: 1px solid #e3e0da; }
    .story-toc li::before { content: none; }
    .story-toc .toc-label { font-size: 0.62rem; }
    .story-toc a { display: block; font-size: 0.78rem; color: #6b727c; padding: 0.32rem 0 0.32rem 0.8rem; margin-left: -1px; border-left: 2px solid transparent; line-height: 1.3; }
    .story-toc a:hover { color: #14181b; text-decoration: none; border-left-color: #c9c3b8; }
  }

  .chip { font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: 0.66rem; letter-spacing: 0.04em; padding: 0.1rem 0.45rem; border-radius: 2px; vertical-align: 0.12em; white-space: nowrap; }
  .chip-record { background: #e7f2ec; color: #22604a; }
  .chip-computed { background: #e8eef4; color: #2b5474; }
  .chip-ok { background: #e7f2ec; color: #22604a; }
  .chip-row { margin: 0.6rem 0 0; }
  .railed { border-left: 3px solid #d8d4cc; padding-left: 1rem; margin: 0.9rem 0; }
  .rail-record { border-left-color: #2e7d5b; }
  .rail-computed { border-left-color: #33658a; }
  .rail-note { border-left-color: #d8d4cc; color: #5b6470; }

  .pattern-box { border: 1px solid #e0d6c2; border-top: 3px solid #c98a1b; background: #fdfaf3; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1rem 0 1.25rem; }
  .p-name { font-family: "Newsreader", Georgia, serif; font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.15rem; margin: 0 0 0.35rem; }
  .p-meaning { margin: 0; color: #3c434b; }

  .howitworked { border: 1px solid #e3e0da; background: #fbfaf8; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.25rem 0; }
  .hw-label, .lane-label, .v-label { font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; color: #5b6470; }
  .howitworked p { margin: 0.4rem 0; }
  .stages { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr auto 1fr auto 1fr; gap: 0.5rem; align-items: start; margin: 0.9rem 0; }
  .stage { border-top: 2px solid #7a9cc4; padding-top: 0.5rem; }
  .s-name { font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #1e4f7a; margin: 0 0 0.3rem; }
  .s-gate { font-family: "Public Sans", system-ui, sans-serif; font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.45; margin: 0; color: #3c434b; }
  .stage-arrow { align-self: center; color: #7a9cc4; font-size: 1.2rem; padding-top: 0.6rem; }
  .note { font-family: "Public Sans", system-ui, sans-serif; font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.55; color: #4f5964; }

